    "body": "#### Recall\n\n**1.** 5\n\n**2.** 3\n\n**3.** 24\n\n**4.** −4\n\n**5.** 0.00408\n\n**6.** 4.5 × 10⁴\n\n**7.** 3/4\n\n**8.** −3\n\n#### Key words\n\n**1.** natural\n\n**2.** integers\n\n**3.** rational\n\n**4.** irrational\n\n**5.** recurring\n\n**6.** terminating\n\n**7.** 22/7 is already written as one integer over another, so it is rational. √2 cannot be written as any such fraction — its decimal never stops and never repeats.\n\n**8.** rational\n\n#### Core practice\n\n**1.** (a) 7 (b) −7 (c) 5 (d) −12\n\n**2.** (a) −35 (b) 24 (c) −6 (d) 4\n\n**3.** −3, −⅔, −½, 0.25\n\n**4.** (a) rational (b) irrational (c) rational (d) irrational (e) rational\n\n**5.** (a) 3/8 (b) 3/5 (c) 1/25\n\n**6.** 3/11\n\n**7.** 1/3\n\n**8.** (a) 47.39 (b) 47.4\n\n**9.** Estimate 400 × 20 ÷ 20 = 400; exactly 7,828 ÷ 21 = 372.8.\n\n**10.** 7 °C\n\n#### Challenge\n\n**1.** Let x = 0.1666…, so 10x = 1.666… and 100x = 16.666…. Subtracting, 90x = 15, so x = 15/90 = 1/6.\n\n**2.** ⅜ = 0.375, which stops. A fraction terminates when the denominator in its simplest form has only 2 and 5 as prime factors; otherwise it recurs.\n\n**3.** (a) −18 + 5 − 9 = −22 °C. (b) A fall of 4 °C. (c) It must rise by 22 °C.\n\n**4.** If 2 + √3 were rational it could be written as a fraction; subtracting the rational 2 would leave √3 as a fraction too, which it is not. So the sum cannot be rational.\n\n\n\n**5.** (a) 47.4. (b) 47. (c) 47. (d) Here they agree, but rounding in stages is still poor practice — with 47.4862 it would give 47 and 47 as well, yet with 44.96 the stages give 45 then 45 while the direct rounding of 44.96 to 2 s.f. is 45; the danger is real whenever the middle digit is a 4 followed by 5 or more.\n\n#### Applied task\n\n**1.** (a) +1,200, −450, −980, +600. (b) 1,200 − 450 − 980 + 600 = GH₵370. (c) 4 × 25 = GH₵100, so the balance becomes GH₵270.\n\n**2.** (a) −36 + 14 − 8 = −30 m. (b) 36 + 14 + 8 = 58 m. (c) Distance counts every movement; the depth counts only the final position. (d) 30 ÷ 3 = 10 minutes.\n\n**3.** (a) 240 − 180 + 320 − 95 + 410 + 150 − 60 = GH₵785. (b) 785 ÷ 7 = GH₵112.14. (c) Three days. (d) The week ended in profit, but she lost money on three of seven days, so the good result rests on two strong days rather than on steady trading.\n\n#### Exam-style\n\n**1.** (a) 5 (b) 24 (c) −4 **[3]**\n\n**2.** −3, −⅔, −½, 0.25 **[3]**\n\n**3.** (a) rational (b) irrational (c) rational **[3]**\n\n**4.** 100x − x = 27 gives 99x = 27, so x = 3/11. **[4]**\n\n**5.** (a) 47.39 (b) 47.4 **[3]**\n\n**6.** (a) −30 m (b) 58 m **[4]**\n\n#### Marking notes and common errors\n\n- **Two signs together.** −(−9) becomes +9. Requiring the intermediate line to be written stops most of the errors in this chapter.\n\n- **Ordering negatives.** −⅔ < −½ although ⅔ > ½. A number line settles every dispute and should be drawn until learners stop needing it.\n\n- **Recurring decimals.** The multiplier matches the number of repeating digits: 10 for one, 100 for two, 1,000 for three.\n\n- **Challenge 2.** The terminating test — only 2s and 5s in the denominator — is beyond the minimum requirement but explains what learners have already noticed.\n\n- **Applied 2(c).** Distance against displacement, met in the bearings chapter, appears again here. The link is worth naming aloud.\n\n\n\n*Strand 1: Number*"
