    "body": "#### Recall\n\n**1.** 3,460\n\n**2.** 45.7\n\n**3.** 0.375\n\n**4.** 3/5\n\n**5.** 124\n\n**6.** 960\n\n**7.** About 20.\n\n**8.** 10.25\n\n#### Key words\n\n**1.** decimal\n\n**2.** dividend\n\n**3.** divisor\n\n**4.** estimate\n\n**5.** recurring\n\n**6.** sum or total\n\n**7.** Dividing asks how many 0.25s fit into the number. Since 0.25 is smaller than 1, many of them fit, so the answer is larger than the number you started with.\n\n**8.** whole\n\n#### Core practice\n\n**1.** (a) 21.15 (b) 13.65\n\n**2.** 8.97\n\n**3.** 0.03\n\n**4.** 12\n\n**5.** 50\n\n**6.** (a) 456 (b) 78 (c) 0.0342\n\n**7.** (a) 0.625 (b) 0.35 (c) 0.455\n\n**8.** (a) 7/8 (b) 6/25\n\n**9.** Estimate 20 × 5 = 100; exactly 96.04.\n\n**10.** 2.5 ÷ 0.35 = 7.14…, so 7 whole pieces using 2.45 m, leaving 0.05 m.\n\n#### Challenge\n\n**1.** The learner has multiplied 6 × 4 correctly but placed the point wrongly. There are two decimal places altogether, so the answer is 0.24. Note also that multiplying two numbers less than 1 must give an answer less than either of them.\n\n**2.** 0.125 is ⅛, and ⅛ of 64 is 8. Recognising the benchmark fraction is far quicker than long multiplication.\n\n**3.** Numerator 6; denominator 0.6; 6 ÷ 0.6 = 10.\n\n**4.** Estimate: 13 × 3.5 ≈ 45, so change about GH₵5. Exactly 12.75 × 3.4 = GH₵43.35, so the change is GH₵6.65.\n\n**5.** Multiplying by 100 moves every digit two places to the left, so the 4 moves from units to hundreds: the answer is 470. Writing 4.700 leaves every digit where it was and only adds zeros after the point, which changes nothing.\n\n\n\n#### Applied task\n\n**1.** (a) 6.5 × 3.4 = 22.1 litres. (b) 22.1 × 14.85 = GH₵328.19 (to the nearest pesewa). (c) 328.185 ÷ 340 = GH₵0.97 a kilometre.\n\n**2.** (a) 12.5 × 38.60 = GH₵482.50. (b) 12.5 ÷ 1.75 = 7.14…, so 7 shirts using 12.25 m, leaving 0.25 m. (c) 7 × 145 = GH₵1,015, so the profit is 1,015 − 482.50 = GH₵532.50.\n\n**3.** (a) 38.5 × 1.85 = GH₵71.23 (to the nearest pesewa). (b) 71.225 + 23.40 = GH₵94.63. (c) New use 33.88 m³, costing GH₵62.68, so the bill is GH₵86.08.\n\n#### Exam-style\n\n**1.** (a) 21.15 (b) 13.65 **[3]**\n\n**2.** 8.97 **[3]**\n\n**3.** 84 ÷ 7 = 12 **[3]**\n\n**4.** (a) 0.625 (b) 7/8 **[3]**\n\n**5.** Estimate 100; exactly 96.04. **[4]**\n\n**6.** (a) GH₵482.50 (b) 7 shirts **[4]**\n\n#### Marking notes and common errors\n\n- **Placing the point.** Count the decimal places in both numbers and put the same number in the answer. The size check — two numbers below 1 give a smaller answer — catches the rest.\n\n- **Dividing by a decimal.** Multiply both numbers until the divisor is whole. Learners who move the point in only one number get an answer ten times out.\n\n- **Estimation.** Every Applied question here should be estimated first. It takes ten seconds and catches the errors that cost whole marks.\n\n- **Core 10 and Applied 2(b).** Part shirts and part pieces do not count. The remainder must be given as a length, not as a decimal fraction of a piece.\n\n- **Money answers.** Two decimal places, and the cedi sign. GH₵328.185 must be written as GH₵328.19 with a note that it has been rounded.\n\n\n\n*Strand 1: Number*"
