    "body": "#### Recall\n\n**1.** 9\n\n**2.** 12\n\n**3.** 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36\n\n**4.** Irrational.\n\n**5.** 3⁶\n\n**6.** x² − 9\n\n**7.** 25\n\n**8.** √2\n\n#### Key words\n\n**1.** surd\n\n**2.** radicand\n\n**3.** like\n\n**4.** rationalising\n\n**5.** a\n\n**6.** √(ab)\n\n**7.** √4 = 2 exactly, so it is a rational number. √5 has no exact fractional value — its decimal never stops or repeats — so it is a surd.\n\n**8.** like\n\n#### Core practice\n\n**1.** (a) 5√2 (b) 6√2 (c) 7√2\n\n**2.** (a) 3√3 (b) 3√5 (c) 6√3\n\n**3.** 2√2 + 3√2 = 5√2\n\n**4.** 4√3\n\n**5.** (a) √36 = 6 (b) √100 = 10\n\n**6.** (a) √9 = 3 (b) √36 = 6\n\n**7.** 5 − 4 = 1\n\n**8.** 3 + 2√3 + 1 = 4 + 2√3\n\n**9.** (a) 2√3 (b) 2√5\n\n**10. (a) rational (4) (b) irrational (c) rational (√16 = 4) (d) irrational (2√3)**\n\n**11. Multiply by 3 − √5: (3 − √5)/(9 − 5) = (3 − √5)/4.**\n\n#### Challenge\n\n**1.** 4√2 + 5√2 − 3√2 = 6√2\n\n**2.** Square roots cannot be added under one sign. √9 + √16 = 3 + 4 = 7, not 5. The rule √a × √b = √(ab) holds for multiplication, never for addition.\n\n**3.** 4/(2√2) = 2/√2 = (2 × √2)/2 = √2.\n\n**4.** (a) √50 = 5√2 cm. (b) 4 × 5√2 = 20√2 cm. (c) 20 × 1.414… = 28.3 cm.\n\n**5.** It is a difference of two squares: 7 − 3 = 4, which is rational. The surd terms cancel.\n\n\n\n#### Applied task\n\n**1.** (a) √(36 + 36) = √72 = 6√2 cm. (b) 8.49 cm. (c) 12 + 6√2 cm.\n\n**2.** (a) √200 = 10√2 m. (b) Diagonal = side × √2 = 10√2 × √2 = 20 m. (c) Perimeter 40√2 ≈ 56.57 m, costing 56.57 × 75 ≈ GH₵4,243.\n\n**3.** (a) √(64 + 64) = √128 = 8√2 m. (b) 11.3 m. (c) The exact surd carries no rounding error, so any later multiplication or squaring stays exact; a rounded value introduces an error that grows with each step.\n\n#### Exam-style\n\n**1.** (a) 5√2 (b) 3√3 **[3]**\n\n**2.** 5√2 **[3]**\n\n**3.** (a) 6 (b) 3 **[3]**\n\n**4.** 1 **[4]**\n\n**5. (a) 2√3 (b) (3 − √5)/4 [3]**\n\n**6.** (a) 5√2 cm (b) 20√2 cm **[4]**\n\n#### Marking notes and common errors\n\n- **Largest square factor.** √50 = 5√2 in one step if 25 is spotted. Learners who use 50 = 2 × 25 in the other order arrive at the same place; those who use 5 × 10 get stuck.\n\n- **Adding under the root sign.** √9 + √16 is not √25. This is the defining error of the chapter and appears every year.\n\n- **Like surds only.** 5√3 − 2√3 works; 5√3 − 2√2 does not simplify at all.\n\n- **Exact answers.** When a question says *exact*, a decimal earns nothing. Learners must leave the surd.\n\n- **Applied 3(c).** The argument for exact values is worth making once properly: rounding early and then squaring multiplies the error.\n\n\n\n*Strand 1: Number*"
