    "body": "#### Recall\n\n**1.** 3/4\n\n**2.** 17/12 or 1 5/12\n\n**3.** 11/6 or 1⅚\n\n**4.** 3/5\n\n**5.** 5/2 or 2½\n\n**6.** 37.5%\n\n**7.** 140\n\n**8.** 6\n\n#### Key words\n\n**1.** reciprocal\n\n**2.** complex or compound\n\n**3.** denominator\n\n**4.** multiple\n\n**5.** 5/4\n\n**6.** 4/5\n\n**7.** Dividing asks how many three-quarters fit into the number. Each whole contains 4/3 of them, so counting them is the same as multiplying by 4/3.\n\n**8.** of\n\n#### Core practice\n\n**1.** 20/24 + 9/24 = 29/24 = 1 5/24\n\n**2.** 21/6 − 8/6 = 13/6 = 2⅙\n\n**3.** 24/36 = ⅔\n\n**4.** ⅚ × 3/10 = 15/60 = ¼\n\n**5.** 9/4 × 4/3 = 3, then 3 ÷ ¾ = 4\n\n**6.** 3/2 = 1½\n\n**7.** 640 × 11/8 = 880\n\n**8.** 880 × 4/5 = 704\n\n**9.** 90 ÷ ⅜ = 90 × 8/3 = 240\n\n**10.** 0.625 and 62.5%\n\n#### Challenge\n\n**1.** ⅝ − ¼ = 3/8 of the tank is 90 litres, so the tank holds 90 × 8/3 = 240 litres.\n\n**2.** The learner has multiplied instead of dividing. Dividing means multiplying by the reciprocal: ⅔ × 4/1 = 8/3 = 2⅔. Note also that the answer must be larger than ⅔.\n\n**3.** 800 × 5/4 = 1,000, then 1,000 × ¾ = GH₵750. The decrease acts on the larger amount, so more is taken off than was put on.\n\n**4.** ⅔ × ¾ × ⅘ = 24/60 = ⅖.\n\n**5.** ⅔ of the girls is 24, so there are 36 girls. Girls are ¾ of the class, so the class has 36 ÷ ¾ = 48 learners.\n\n\n\n#### Applied task\n\n**1.** (a) ⅜ × 240 = 90 kg. (b) 150 kg. (c) ⅖ × 150 = 60 kg. (d) 90 kg remain, which is 90/240 = ⅜ of the original.\n\n**2.** (a) 1 − ⅕ − ⅖ = ⅖ stone. (b) Cement 7½ × ⅕ = 1½ t; sand 7½ × ⅖ = 3 t; stone 3 t. (c) 1.5 × 1,050 + 3 × 260 = 1,575 + 780 = GH₵2,355.\n\n**3.** (a) ⅜ × 1,600 = GH₵600. (b) Remainder GH₵1,000; ¼ of it is GH₵250. (c) 1,000 − 250 = GH₵750. (d) 750/1,600 = 15/32.\n\n#### Exam-style\n\n**1.** 1 5/24 **[3]**\n\n**2.** (a) ⅔ (b) ¼ **[4]**\n\n**3.** 1½ **[4]**\n\n**4.** 880, then 704 **[3]**\n\n**5.** 240 litres **[3]**\n\n**6.** (a) GH₵600 (b) GH₵750 **[3]**\n\n#### Marking notes and common errors\n\n- **Mixed numbers to improper fractions** before any multiplication or division, without exception.\n\n- **Fraction of the remainder.** In every one of the Applied questions the second fraction acts on what is left, not on the original. This is the single most common misreading.\n\n- **Core 9 and Challenge 1.** Finding the whole from a part means dividing by the fraction. Learners who multiply get an answer smaller than the part, which is impossible.\n\n- **Challenge 3.** Increase then decrease by the same fraction does not return to the start; the two fractions act on different amounts.\n\n- **Simplest form.** An unsimplified but correct answer normally loses one mark. Say so before the exercise, not after.\n\n\n\n*Strand 1: Number*"
