    "body": "#### Recall\n\n**1.** 2\n\n**2.** 7\n\n**3.** x = 6\n\n**4.** x = 5\n\n**5.** 3x − 6\n\n**6.** 7\n\n**7.** 4\n\n**8.** x + 4y\n\n#### Key words\n\n**1.** simultaneous\n\n**2.** intersection\n\n**3.** substitution\n\n**4.** elimination\n\n**5.** parallel\n\n**6.** no\n\n**7.** It gives the one pair of values of x and y that makes both equations true at the same time — the solution of the pair.\n\n**8.** both\n\n#### Core practice\n\n**1.** y = x + 1: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6. y = 7 − x: 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2.\n\n**2.** They cross at (3, 4).\n\n**3.** x = 3, y = 4\n\n**4.** x = 4, y = 2\n\n**5.** x = 4, y = 3\n\n**6.** Adding gives 8x = 24, x = 3, y = 5.5\n\n**7.** Adding gives 6x = 24, x = 4, y = ⅔\n\n✓\n\n**8.** 3 + 4 = 7 ✓ and 6 − 4 = 2\n\n**9.** Both have gradient 2, so the lines are parallel and never cross.\n\n**10.** x + y = 25 and x − y = 7 give x = 16 and y = 9.\n\n#### Challenge\n\n**1.** Multiply the first by 3 and the second by 2: 9x + 6y = 36 and 10x − 6y = 2. Adding gives 19x = 38, so x = 2 and then y = 3.\n\n**2.** A wrong pair can still satisfy one equation by chance. For x + y = 7 and 2x − y = 2, the pair x = 5, y = 2 satisfies neither properly: 5 + 2 = 7 ✓ but 10 − 2 = 8 ✗. Only the second check exposes the error.\n\n**3.** (a) Both 3. (b) Setting them equal gives −4 = 1, which is impossible. (c) The lines are parallel and never meet, so there is no solution.\n\n**4.** Multiply the first by 6: 3x + 2y = 24. From the second x = y + 2, so 3y + 6 + 2y = 24, giving y = 3.6 and x = 5.6.\n\n**5.** Any pair satisfied by (2, 5), for example x + y = 7 and y − x = 3: 2 + 5 = ✓7 and 5 − 2 = 3 ✓.\n\n\n\n#### Applied task\n\n**1.** (a) 3b + 2p = 34 and 5b + 2p = 46. (b) Subtracting gives 2b = 12, so b = GH₵6 and p = GH₵8. (c) 4(6) + 5(8) = GH₵64.\n\n**2.** (a) a + c = 25 and 8a + 5c = 170. (b) Substituting c = 25 − a gives 3a = 45, so a = 15 and c = 10. (c) Each swap adds GH₵3, so three swaps add GH₵9.\n\n**3.** (a) 2(l + w) = 46 and l = w + 5. (b) l + w = 23, so w + 5 + w = 23, giving w = 9 and l = 14. (c) 126 cm². (d) √(196 + 81) = √277 ≈ 16.6 cm.\n\n#### Exam-style\n\n**1.** x = 3, y = 4 **[4]**\n\n**2.** x = 4, y = 2 **[4]**\n\n**3.** The two lines cross at (3, 4). **[4]**\n\n**4.** (3, 4); the pair of values satisfying both equations at once. **[3]**\n\n**5.** Equal gradients, so the lines are parallel and never cross. **[2]**\n\n**6.** b = GH₵6 and p = GH₵8 **[3]**\n\n#### Marking notes and common errors\n\n- **Check in both equations.** A pair that satisfies one equation may satisfy neither properly. This is the single most useful habit in the chapter.\n\n- **Choosing a method.** Elimination when a variable already has matching or opposite coefficients; substitution when one equation is already solved for a variable.\n\n- **Graphical solutions** are only as accurate as the drawing. Where a question says *solve graphically*, the reading must still be stated as a coordinate pair.\n\n- **Challenge 4.** Clearing fractions first, by multiplying through by the lowest common denominator, saves the whole question.\n\n- **Applied 2(c).** The three-cedi difference for each swap is a neat insight and worth drawing out; learners who recalculate the whole problem still earn full marks.\n\n\n\n*Strand 2: Algebra*"
