    "body": "#### Recall\n\n**1.** 6x − 15\n\n**2.** 4x(2x + 3)\n\n**3.** (x − 9)(x + 9)\n\n**4.** (x + 3)(x + 4)\n\n**5.** x = 6\n\n**6.** 96 cm²\n\n**7.** 11\n\n**8.** 8\n\n#### Key words\n\n**1.** subject\n\n**2.** subject\n\n**3.** inverse\n\n**4.** factorising\n\n**5.** squares\n\n**6.** grouping\n\n**7.** Both use inverse operations to isolate one letter. Solving works with numbers and ends with a value; changing the subject works with letters and ends with a formula.\n\n**8.** other\n\n#### Core practice\n\n**1.** w = A/l\n\n**2.** t = (v − u)/a\n\n**3.** h = 2A/b\n\n**4.** r = C/(2π)\n\n**5.** x = (y + 7)/3\n\n**6.** u = (s − ½at²)/t\n\n**7.** t = 7\n\n**8.** (a) 6x(2x − 3) (b) 5a(b + 2a)\n\n**9.** (a) (x − 11)(x + 11) (b) (3x − 4)(3x + 4)\n\n**10.** (a) (x − 5)(x − 6) (b) (x + 5)(x − 3)\n\n#### Challenge\n\n**1.** r = √(A/π). With A = 154: r² = 154 × 7 ÷ 22 = 49, so r = 7.\n\n**2.** (x + y)(3 + a)\n\n**3.** Two numbers multiplying to 6 and adding to 7 are 6 and 1. 2x² + 6x + x + 3 = 2x(x + 3) + 1(x + 3) = (x + 3)(2x + 1).\n\n**4.** y(x − 1) = x + 3, so xy − y = x + 3, then xy − x = y + 3, x(y − 1) = y + 3 and x = (y + 3)/(y − 1).\n\n**5.** Without brackets the division applies only to u, so the expression means v − (u ÷ a). The whole of v − u must be divided by a: t = (v − u)/a.\n\n#### Applied task\n\n\n\n**1.** (a) R = 100I/(PT). (b) R = 108,000 ÷ 13,500 = 8%. (c) T = 100I/(PR) = 90,000 ÷ 30,000 = 3 years.\n\n**2.** (a) r = √(A/π). (b) r² = 1,386 × 7 ÷ 22 = 441, so r = 21 m. (c) C = 2 × 22/7 × 21 = 132 m. (d) 132 × 65 = GH₵8,580.\n\n**3.** (a) (x + 5)(x − 3). (b) Length (x + 5) m and width (x − 3) m. (c) 13 m and 5 m. (d) 2(13 + 5) = 36 m.\n\n#### Exam-style\n\n**1.** t = (v − u)/a **[3]**\n\n**2.** h = 2A/b = 96 ÷ 12 = 8 **[4]**\n\n**3.** (a) 6x(2x − 3) (b) (3x − 4)(3x + 4) **[4]**\n\n**4.** (x − 5)(x − 6) **[3]**\n\n**5.** r = √(A/π) = 7 **[3]**\n\n**6.** (x + y)(3 + a) **[3]**\n\n#### Marking notes and common errors\n\n- **Brackets when dividing.** t = (v − u)/a needs the brackets. Without them the formula means something else, and this is where the marks go.\n\n- **Check with numbers.** Every rearranged formula can be tested by substituting values that fit the original. Teach it as the standard check.\n\n- **Roots and squares.** To undo a square, take the square root of *both sides*; to undo a root, square both sides.\n\n- **Challenge 4.** Collecting the x terms on one side is the whole method, and it returns in senior high school. Reward a correct method even where the final algebra slips.\n\n- **Factorising.** Always check by expanding. A learner who does this cannot hand in a wrong factorisation.\n\n\n\n*Strand 2: Algebra*"
