    "body": "#### Recall\n\n**1.** 95°\n\n**2.** 65°\n\n**3.** 360°\n\n**4.** 72°\n\n**5.** 135°\n\n**6.** 44 cm\n\n**7.** The diameter.\n\n**8.** 130\n\n#### Key words\n\n**1.** chord\n\n**2.** arc\n\n**3.** segment\n\n**4.** sector\n\n**5.** cyclic\n\n**6.** tangent\n\n**7.** The angle subtended at the centre by an arc is twice the angle subtended by the same arc at any point on the remaining circumference.\n\n**8.** 90°\n\n#### Core practice\n\n**1.** 65°, because the angle at the centre is twice the angle at the circumference.\n\n**2.** 84°\n\n**3.** 90°, because the angle in a semicircle is a right angle.\n\n**4.** 95°, because opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral add to 180°.\n\n**5.** 70°\n\n**6.** 38°, because angles in the same segment are equal.\n\n**7.** 55°\n\n**8.** OA = OB (radii), so the triangle is isosceles and the base angles are (180 − 96) ÷ 2 = 42° each.\n\n**9.** It measures 90°.\n\n**10.** Angle at centre is twice angle at circumference; angle in a semicircle is 90°; angles in the same segment are equal; opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral add to 180°.\n\n#### Challenge\n\n**1.** The angle at the centre is twice that at the circumference, so 3x − 40 = 2x, giving x = 40°.\n\n**2.** 2x + x + 30 = 180, so 3x = 150 and x = 50. Angle A = 100° and angle C = 80°.\n\n**3.** (a) 90°, the angle in a semicircle. (b) √(676 − 100) = 24 cm. (c) ½ × 10 × 24 = 120 cm².\n\n**4.** Opposite angles of a parallelogram are equal, and opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral add to 180°. Both can be true only if each pair is 90°, which makes it a rectangle.\n\n**5.** Angle ACB = 55° (angle at centre is twice angle at circumference). Triangle OAB is isosceles because OA and OB are radii, so angle OAB = (180 − 110) ÷ 2 = 35°.\n\n\n\n#### Applied task\n\n**1.** (a) 90°, the angle in a semicircle. (b) 180 − 90 − 28 = 62°. (c) 2 × 22/7 × 35 = 220 cm.\n\n**2.** (a) 3x + x + 60 = 180, so 4x = 120 and x = 30. (b) 90° and 90°. (c) 2 × 22/7 × 70 = 440 cm.\n\n**3.** (a) 48°, because the angle at the centre is twice the angle at the circumference. (b) Isosceles with base angles 42° each and 96° at O. (c) Arc = 96/360 × 2 × 22/7 × 14 = 23.47 m (to 2 d.p.).\n\n#### Exam-style\n\n**1.** 65°, angle at centre is twice the angle at the circumference. **[3]**\n\n**2.** 90°, angle in a semicircle. **[2]**\n\n**3.** C = 95° and D = 70°, because opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral add to 180°. **[4]**\n\n**4.** 42° each, since OA = OB makes the triangle isosceles. **[4]**\n\n**5.** 3x − 40 = 2x, so x = 40°. **[4]**\n\n**6.** 24 cm **[3]**\n\n#### Marking notes and common errors\n\n- **Reasons.** Every circle-angle answer needs its theorem named. The number alone earns half the marks at best.\n\n- **Radii make isosceles triangles.** Any triangle with two sides as radii is isosceles, and this unlocks most of the harder questions.\n\n- **Angle at the centre.** Doubling and halving in the wrong direction is the common slip: the centre angle is always the larger.\n\n- **Challenge 4.** A neat piece of reasoning that connects two topics; accept any argument that reaches the rectangle conclusion.\n\n- **Applied 3(c).** The arc length formula (θ/360) × 2πr is worth stating on the board before this question.\n\n\n\n*Strand 3: Geometry and Measurement*"
