    "body": "#### Recall\n\n**1.** 40 cm²\n\n**2.** 12 cm²\n\n**3.** 44 cm\n\n**4.** 154 cm²\n\n**5.** 245°\n\n**6.** 5\n\n**7.** 120 cm³\n\n**8.** 045°\n\n#### Key words\n\n**1.** prism\n\n**2.** faces\n\n**3.** edge\n\n**4.** surface\n\n**5.** rectangle\n\n**6.** bearing\n\n**7.** Cutting the tube straight down its side and rolling it flat gives a rectangle whose length is the circumference of the circle and whose width is the height of the cylinder.\n\n**8.** resultant\n\n#### Core practice\n\n**1.** 158 cm²\n\n**2.** 120 cm³\n\n**3.** (a) ½ × 3 × 4 = 6 cm² (b) 6 × 12 = 72 cm³\n\n**4.** Two triangles 2 × 6 = 12 cm²; three rectangles (3 + 4 + 5) × 12 = 144 cm²; total 156 cm².\n\n**5.** 440 cm²\n\n**6.** 748 cm²\n\n**7.** 22/7 × 49 × 10 = 1,540 cm³\n\n**8.** (a) 335° (b) 115°\n\n**9.** 13 km\n\n**10.** Scalars: mass, distance, speed. Vectors: velocity, displacement, force.\n\n#### Challenge\n\n**1.** (a) 2 × 22/7 × 7 × 4 + 2 × 22/7 × 49 = 176 + 308 = 484 m². (b) 484 × 45 = GH₵21,780.\n\n**2.** SA = 2x² + 40x = 768, so x² + 20x − 384 = 0, giving (x + 32)(x − 12) = 0 and x = 12 cm (the negative root is rejected because a length cannot be negative).\n\n**3.** (a) 4 cm then 3 cm, each from a fresh north line. (b) The legs are at right angles, so the distance is √(64 + 36) = 10 km. (c) About 097°.\n\n**4.** Length 200 cm; curved area = 2 × 22/7 × 3.5 × 200 = 4,400 cm².\n\n**5.** Surface area depends on two dimensions, so each face becomes 2 × 2 = 4 times as large. Volume depends on three, so it becomes 2 × 2 × 2 = 8 times as large.\n\n\n\n#### Applied task\n\n**1.** (a) 9 m³. (b) 9,000 litres. (c) 2(6 + 4.5 + 3) = 27 m². (d) 27 × 38 = GH₵1,026.\n\n**2.** (a) 22/7 × 0.49 × 1.2 = 1.848 m³. (b) 1,848 litres. (c) 2 × 22/7 × 0.7 × 1.2 + 2 × 22/7 × 0.49 = 5.28 + 3.08 = 8.36 m². (d) 8.36 × 120 = GH₵1,003.20.\n\n**3.** (a) 3 cm then 4 cm. (b) 130 − 40 = 90°, so the legs are perpendicular. (c) √(81 + 144) = 15 km. (d) The outward bearing is about 093°, so the return bearing is about 273°.\n\n#### Exam-style\n\n**1.** 158 cm² **[3]**\n\n**2.** 440 cm² and 748 cm² **[4]**\n\n**3.** 72 cm³ and 156 cm² **[4]**\n\n**4.** 335° and 115° **[3]**\n\n**5.** 13 km **[3]**\n\n**6.** 484 m² at GH₵45 = GH₵21,780 **[3]**\n\n#### Marking notes and common errors\n\n- **Surface area against volume.** Surface area is in square units, volume in cubic units. The units alone often show which formula a learner has used.\n\n- **Cylinders.** Curved surface 2πrh; each end πr²; total 2πrh + 2πr². A pipe open at both ends has only the curved surface, and questions say so deliberately.\n\n- **Triangular prisms.** Two triangles plus three rectangles. Learners frequently forget one rectangle; counting the faces first prevents it.\n\n- **Bearings.** Three figures, measured clockwise from north, and a fresh north line at every turning point.\n\n- **Challenge 5.** The square and cube scaling result is worth stating explicitly; it explains why large animals are not simply scaled-up small ones.\n\n\n\n*Strand 3: Geometry and Measurement*"
