    "body": "#### Recall\n\n**1.** (−3, −2)\n\n**2.** (−3, 2)\n\n**3.** 12\n\n**4.** 6\n\n**5.** 24 cm²\n\n**6.** 9\n\n**7.** 2 cm\n\n**8.** 3 : 1\n\n#### Key words\n\n**1.** enlargement\n\n**2.** factor\n\n**3.** enlargement\n\n**4.** smaller\n\n**5.** similar\n\n**6.** stay the same\n\n**7.** Every length is multiplied by 3, and area comes from two lengths multiplied together, so the area is multiplied by 3 × 3 = 9.\n\n**8.** rays\n\n#### Core practice\n\n**1.** (6, 3)\n\n**2.** (4, −3)\n\n**3.** 3\n\n**4.** ⅓\n\n**5.** A ′(2, 2), B ′(6, 2), C ′(2, 4)\n\n**6.** (a) 12 cm by 9 cm (b) 108 cm² (c) 9\n\n**7.** The shape and the angles stay the same; the lengths — and therefore the area — change.\n\n**8.** 25 cm by 20 cm\n\n**9.** 450 ÷ 25 = 18 cm\n\n**10.** 20 × 16 = 320 cm²\n\n#### Challenge\n\n**1.** (a) (1, 1), (3, 1), (1, 2.5). (b) Object ½ × 4 × 3 = 6 cm²; image ½ × 2 × 1.5 = 1.5 cm². (c) Area factor ¼ = (½)². ✓\n\n**2.** 6, because every length is first tripled and then doubled: 3 × 2 = 6.\n\n**3.** (a) 4,600 ÷ 200 = 23 cm. (b) 1.5 × 200 = 300 cm = 3 m. (c) Area factor 200² = 40,000, so 900 × 40,000 = 36,000,000 cm² = 3,600 m².\n\n**4.** A 3 cm by 2 cm rectangle has area 6 cm². Enlarged by 2 it becomes 6 cm by 4 cm with area 24 cm² — four times as large, not twice.\n\n**5.** (a) 8 ÷ 2 = 4 and 12 ÷ 3 = 4, so the scale factor is 4. (b) The origin, because multiplying both coordinates by the same factor is exactly what an enlargement about (0, 0) does.\n\n\n\n#### Applied task\n\n**1.** (a) 900 ÷ 50 = 18 cm. (b) 2.4 × 50 = 120 cm = 1.2 m by 0.6 m. (c) 1.2 × 0.6 = 0.72 m². (d) 18 cm by 12 cm = 216 cm².\n\n**2.** (a) 24 ÷ 15 = 1.6. (b) 10 × 1.6 = 16 cm. (c) 150 cm² and 384 cm². (d) 384 ÷ 150 = 2.56, and 1.6² = 2.56. ✓\n\n**3.** (a) 0.07 m and 0.12 m. (b) 22/7 × 0.49 × 1.2 = 1.848 m³. (c) 22/7 × 0.0049 × 0.12 = 0.001848 m³. (d) The volume factor is 1/1,000, which is (1/10)³ — volume involves three lengths, so the scale factor is cubed.\n\n#### Exam-style\n\n**1.** (6, 3) and (4, −3) **[3]**\n\n**2.** 3 **[2]**\n\n**3.** A ′(2, 2), B ′(6, 2), C ′(2, 4) **[4]**\n\n**4.** 108 cm²; area factor 9 **[4]**\n\n**5.** 18 cm **[3]**\n\n**6.** A 3 cm by 2 cm rectangle of area 6 cm² becomes 6 cm by 4 cm with area 24 cm² — four times as large. **[4]**\n\n#### Marking notes and common errors\n\n- **Two ingredients.** Scale factor and centre. A description giving only the scale factor is incomplete.\n\n- **Area and volume factors.** Area is multiplied by k², volume by k³. This is examined every year and is the single most useful idea in the chapter.\n\n- **Fractional scale factors** still count as enlargements even though the image is smaller. Learners often call these reductions; the mathematical name is enlargement with k < 1.\n\n- **Core 5 and Exam 3.** The origin, the object point and the image point must lie on one straight line. Drawing the rays is the quickest check.\n\n- **Applied 3(d).** The cube law for volume surprises learners and is worth a demonstration with centimetre cubes.\n\n\n\n### Review 3 — Geometry and Measurement\n\n*Chapters 9 to 11. Time: 45 minutes. Total: 30 marks.*\n\n**1.** 65°, angle at centre is twice the angle at the circumference. **[3]**\n\n**2.** C = 95°, D = 70°, because opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral add to 180°. **[4]**\n\n**3.** 42° each **[3]**\n\n**4.** 158 cm² **[3]**\n\n**5.** 748 cm² **[4]**\n\n**6.** 72 cm³ **[3]**\n\n**7.** 335° and 115° **[2]**\n\n**8.** 13 km **[3]**\n\n**9.** (6, 3) and (4, −3) **[2]**\n\n**10.** 108 cm²; area factor 9 **[3]**\n\n\n\n*Strand 4: Handling Data*"
