    "body": "#### Recall\n\n**1.** 13\n\n**2.** 5\n\n**3.** 4\n\n**4.** 23\n\n**5.** 45\n\n**6.** 44.5\n\n**7.** A histogram has no gaps between the bars.\n\n**8.** 540\n\n#### Key words\n\n**1.** frequency\n\n**2.** boundaries\n\n**3.** midpoint\n\n**4.** modal\n\n**5.** range\n\n**6.** polygon\n\n**7.** When there are extreme values. One very large or very small value pulls the mean towards it, while the median stays in the middle of the data and is not affected.\n\n**8.** values\n\n#### Core practice\n\n**1.** 744 ÷ 15 = 49.6\n\n**2.** (a) 49 (b) 23\n\n**3.** 3, 8, 4; total 15.\n\n**4.** 39.5, 49.5 and 59.5\n\n**5.** 752.5 ÷ 15 = 50.17 (to 2 d.p.)\n\n**6.** 45–54\n\n**7.** Three touching bars of heights 3, 8 and 4, with labelled axes and a title.\n\n**8.** A line joining (39.5, 3), (49.5, 8) and (59.5, 4), extended to the midpoints of the empty classes on either side.\n\n**9.** Old total 200; new total 290 over 11 values, giving 26.36.\n\n**10.** The median, GH₵430. The one very large wage pulls the mean up to GH₵938, which is higher than four of the five wages.\n\n#### Challenge\n\n**1.** Total must be 144; the five given add to 118, so the sixth is 26.\n\n**2.** Grouping replaces every value by its class midpoint, so information is lost. Here the estimate is 50.17 and the true mean 49.6 — the difference arises because the values in the middle class sit below its midpoint.\n\n**3.** Class total 1,860; boys' total 1,044; girls' total 816 over 12 girls, giving a mean of 68.\n\n**4.** The mean says where the centre is; the range says how spread out the values are. 49, 50, 51 and 10, 50, 90 both have mean 50, but the second is far more variable.\n\n\n\n**5.** Total frequency 20 + x. Sum of products 75 + 25x + 315 + 270 = 660 + 25x. (660 + 25x) ÷ (20 + x) = 31 gives 660 + 25x = 620 + 31x, so 6x = 40 and x = 6.67 — not a whole number, so no whole-number frequency gives exactly 31; the nearest is x = 7.\n\n#### Applied task\n\n**1.** Marked on method: equal class widths covering all marks, a histogram with touching bars and labelled axes, an estimated mean from midpoints, and conclusions that follow from the data rather than from impression.\n\n**2.** (a) Mean 49.6 kg, median 49 kg, range 23 kg. (b) 3, 8, 4. (c) 50.17 kg. (d) 744 × 6.20 = GH₵4,612.80 exactly, or 752.5 × 6.20 = GH₵4,665.50 from the grouped estimate.\n\n**3.** (a) 40–59. (b) Midpoints 9.5, 29.5, 49.5, 69.5, 89.5 give (28.5 + 236 + 544.5 + 417 + 179) ÷ 30 = 1,405 ÷ 30 = 46.83. (c) 11 days. (d) Only 8 of the 30 days reached 60 or more, so the claim is wrong: sales exceed 60 on about a quarter of days.\n\n#### Exam-style\n\n**1.** Mean 49.6; range 23. **[4]**\n\n**2.** 3, 8, 4; total 15. **[3]**\n\n**3.** 39.5, 49.5, 59.5; mean 752.5 ÷ 15 = 50.17. **[4]**\n\n**4.** Three touching bars of heights 3, 8 and 4. **[3]**\n\n**5.** 26 **[3]**\n\n**6.** The median, GH₵430, because the one very large wage raises the mean above four of the five values. **[3]**\n\n#### Marking notes and common errors\n\n- **Class boundaries.** For 35–44 and 45–54 the boundaries are 34.5 and 44.5, and the midpoint is 39.5. Learners who use 39 or 40 lose accuracy in every later calculation.\n\n- **Estimated mean.** The word *estimate* belongs in the answer, and the midpoints must be shown.\n\n- **Outliers.** One extreme value pulls the mean but not the median. This is the most examined idea in the chapter.\n\n- **Challenge 5** deliberately has no whole-number answer. A learner who says so has understood more than one who forces an answer.\n\n- **Applied 3(d).** Test the claim against the table, not against impression. Eight days out of thirty is not *usually*.\n\n\n\n*Strand 4: Handling Data*"
