    "body": "#### Recall\n\n**1.** 5/8\n\n**2.** ¼\n\n**3.** ¼\n\n**4.** 5/14\n\n**5.** 25/28\n\n**6.** 5/14\n\n**7.** 11/36\n\n**8.** 7\n\n#### Key words\n\n**1.** dependent\n\n**2.** independent\n\n**3.** replacement\n\n**4.** replacement\n\n**5.** tree\n\n**6.** multiplied\n\n**7.** The item taken is not put back, so both the number of favourable items and the total change. The second probability therefore depends on what happened first.\n\n**8.** added\n\n#### Core practice\n\n**1.** 5/8 × 4/7 = 5/14\n\n**2.** 3/8 × 2/7 = 6/56 = 3/28\n\n**3.** 15/56 + 15/56 = 30/56 = 15/28\n\n**4.** 1 − 3/28 = 25/28\n\n**5.** 5/14 + 3/28 + 15/28 = 10/28 + 3/28 + 15/28 = 28/28 = 1 ✓\n\n**6.** (a) independent (b) dependent (c) independent (d) dependent\n\n**7.** 4/6 × 3/5 = 12/30 = 2/5\n\n**8.** 2/6 × 1/5 = 1/15\n\n**9.** 1 − 2/5 = 3/5\n\n**10.** First stage 5/8 and 3/8; second stage 4/7 and 3/7 after red, 5/7 and 2/7 after blue.\n\n#### Challenge\n\n**1.** (a) 12/20 × 11/19 = 132/380 = 33/95. (b) (12/20 × 8/19) + (8/20 × 12/19) = 96/380 + 96/380 = 192/380 = 48/95.\n\n**2.** With replacement 25/64 = 0.391; without replacement 5/14 = 0.357. Removing a red leaves fewer reds in a smaller bag, so the second probability falls.\n\n**3.** The denominator must fall to 7, because one bead has been removed and only seven remain. Keeping 8 treats the draws as if the bead had been replaced.\n\n**4.** 5/8 × 4/7 × 3/6 = 60/336 = 5/28.\n\n**5.** (a) 7/10 × 6/9 = 42/90 = 7/15. (b) 1 − 7/15 = 8/15. (c) The box is rejected more often than not even though only 30% of the items are faulty, so the test is harsh on the seller — though it protects the buyer.\n\n\n\n#### Applied task\n\n**1.** (a) 33/95. (b) 48/95. (c) 1 − 33/95 = 62/95. (d) Random choice is fair in the sense that everyone has the same chance, but it does not guarantee that both sexes are represented — which may matter more.\n\n**2.** (a) 15/20 × 14/19 = 210/380 = 21/38 ≈ 0.553. (b) 1 − 21/38 = 17/38 ≈ 0.447. (c) 75/100 × 74/99 = 5,550/9,900 ≈ 0.561, slightly higher: with a larger stock, removing one phone changes the proportion less.\n\n**3.** (a) First stage 6/10 and 4/10; second stage 5/9 and 4/9 after A, 6/9 and 3/9 after B. (b) 6/10 × 5/9 = 30/90 = ⅓. (c) (6/10 × 4/9) + (4/10 × 6/9) = 24/90 + 24/90 = 48/90 = 8/15. (d) The first dose is not replaced, so both the number of that type and the total fall.\n\n#### Exam-style\n\n**1.** 5/8 × 4/7 = 5/14 **[3]**\n\n**2.** 15/28 **[4]**\n\n**3.** 25/28 **[3]**\n\n**4.** Dependent: the first bead is not replaced, so the numbers available for the second draw change. **[3]**\n\n**5.** 2/5 and 3/5 **[4]**\n\n**6.** 33/95 **[3]**\n\n#### Marking notes and common errors\n\n- **The falling denominator.** 8 then 7, 10 then 9. A learner whose second denominator is unchanged has treated the events as independent.\n\n- **One of each.** Two paths, both counted, then added. Halving the answer or taking only one order is the standard error.\n\n- **At least one.** Through the complement every time.\n\n- **Checking.** All the outcomes of a tree diagram must add to 1. This catches almost every arithmetic slip.\n\n- **Applied 2(c).** A genuinely interesting result: with a large stock, drawing without replacement behaves almost like drawing with it.\n\n\n\n### Review 4 — Handling Data\n\n*Chapters 12 and 13. Time: 40 minutes. Total: 30 marks.*\n\n**1.** Mean 49.6; range 23. **[4]**\n\n**2.** 3, 8, 4; total 15. **[3]**\n\n**3.** 39.5, 49.5, 59.5; mean 752.5 ÷ 15 = 50.17. **[4]**\n\n**4.** Three touching bars of heights 3, 8 and 4. **[3]**\n\n**5.** 26 **[3]**\n\n**6.** The median, GH₵430, because one very large wage raises the mean above four of the five values. **[3]**\n\n**7.** 5/14 **[3]**\n\n**8.** 15/28 **[3]**\n\n**9.** 25/28 **[2]**\n\n**10.** The first bead is not returned, so both the favourable count and the total change for the second draw. **[2]**"
